// @korulang/unikraft-lock — Unikraft's `uklock`, lifted at the one place it can
// honestly be lifted: the READER-WRITER LOCK.
//
//   import unikraft/lock
//
// WHAT `uklock` ACTUALLY EXPORTS, and why this module is a rwlock module rather
// than a lock module. `lib/uklock/exportsyms.uk` exists, so the brief's case 1
// applies: that file IS the allowlist and nothing else links. It has 15 lines.
// Read as a linker reads it rather than as a header reads it, those 15 lines
// contain exactly ONE complete, callable API:
//
//   * `uk_rwlock_init_config`, `_rlock`, `_wlock`, `_runlock`, `_wunlock` —
//     five real functions in `rwlock.c`, the whole reader-writer surface. THIS
//     MODULE.
//   * `uk_rwlock_upgrade`, `uk_rwlock_downgrade` — **on the allowlist and
//     nowhere else in the tree.** `grep -rn "rwlock_upgrade\|rwlock_downgrade"`
//     over all of `unikraft` at HEAD 3fdffba8 returns exactly two hits, both in
//     `exportsyms.uk` itself. There is no declaration, no definition, no caller.
//     The allowlist promises two symbols the library does not have. An
//     allowlist is not an inventory.
//   * `uk_mutex_init_config`, `uk_semaphore_init` — constructors whose
//     OPERATIONS are `static inline` (`mutex.h:163` `uk_mutex_lock`, `:186`
//     `uk_mutex_trylock`, `:227` `uk_mutex_unlock`; `semaphore.h:53`
//     `uk_semaphore_down`, `:157` `uk_semaphore_up`, and four more). A lift of
//     `uk_mutex_init_config` alone hands a caller a mutex it can never lock.
//     REFUSED — see README.md, "Claims I do not make".
//   * `_uk_mutex_lock_wait`, `_uk_mutex_unlock_wait`, `_uk_mutex_trylock_wait` —
//     the SLOW-PATH HALVES of those inlines. `_uk_mutex_lock_wait(m, tid, v)`
//     is entered only after the caller's compare-exchange has already failed
//     and `v` holds the observed word; calling it standalone is not "lock", it
//     is the contended branch with its precondition unmet. REFUSED.
//   * `uk_mutex_get_metrics`, `_uk_mutex_metrics`, `_uk_mutex_metrics_lock` —
//     `CONFIG_LIBUKLOCK_MUTEX_METRICS` is `default n` (`Config.uk:41`), so in a
//     stock image these three are on the allowlist and absent from the binary.
//
// So the shelf's "15 linkable" is 5 usable functions, 2 phantoms, 3 slow-path
// halves, 3 metrics symbols that are usually not there, and 2 constructors with
// no reachable verbs. That is the `uknetdev` trap in another library: the
// lifecycle exports and the operations do not. Here the reader-writer lock is
// the half that survives it whole, and it is also the half with the ordering
// rule worth lifting.
//
// THE ORDERING RULE, and it is a real one. `uk_rwlock_runlock` and
// `uk_rwlock_wunlock` take the SAME argument, return the same void, and are
// spelled four characters apart. They are not interchangeable:
//
//     rwlock.c:98   UK_ASSERT(rwl->nactive > 0)    // runlock: a READ lock is held
//     rwlock.c:120  UK_ASSERT(rwl->nactive == -1)  // wunlock: a WRITE lock is held
//
// Cross them and, with `CONFIG_LIBUKDEBUG_ENABLE_ASSERT` off — which is every
// image anyone ships — nothing objects and the lock is destroyed:
//
//   * `wlock` then `runlock` drives `nactive` from -1 to **-2**. From there
//     `nactive >= 0` is false forever, so no reader ever enters again, and
//     `nactive == 0` is false forever, so no writer does either. The lock is
//     permanently dead and the program hangs at the next acquire, arbitrarily
//     far from the mistake.
//   * `rlock` then `wunlock` drives `nactive` from 1 to **0**. The lock now
//     reads as free while a reader is inside it, so the next writer is admitted
//     concurrently with that reader. Silent data race, no hang, no diagnostic.
//
// That is the assertion pair this module makes structural, and it is the reason
// the two hold-modes are two phantom states rather than one `held`.
//
// THE RATCHET. One handle, four states:
//
//     create         mints <fresh!>                       uk_rwlock_init_config(l, 0)
//     read.acquire   needs <!fresh|!idle>  mints <reading!>  uk_rwlock_rlock
//     read.release   needs <!reading>      mints <idle!>     uk_rwlock_runlock
//     write.acquire  needs <!fresh|!idle>  mints <writing!>  uk_rwlock_wlock
//     write.release  needs <!writing>      mints <idle!>     uk_rwlock_wunlock
//     hold.skipped   needs <!fresh>        mints <idle!>     NO C CALL — the escape
//     destroy        needs <!idle>                          free (uklock has no destructor)
//
// The mode is the first token of the tor name on purpose: `grep -r "lock:write"`
// answers "where does this program take the write lock", which is the question
// a reader of concurrent code actually asks and which `unlock` cannot answer.
//
// THE ASYMMETRY (pillar 4). `destroy` accepts ONLY <!idle>, and <idle!> is
// minted only by a release — that is, only after the lock was really taken. So
//
//     create(locks) | lock l |> destroy(lock: l)
//
// does not compile. This is the shape `2104_14_open_tx_commit_close/db.kz` uses
// (`close` takes <!active> and not <!connected>) and gzip's `fed` gate
// (`koru-libs/gzip/index.kz:258`). The escape is named and greppable —
// `lock:hold.skipped` — exactly as `blk:io.skipped` and `alloc:untouched` are,
// because a lock nobody ever takes is a live allocation and a design question,
// and the honest program should be able to say so in one word.
//
// WHERE I DECIDED THE C HAS NO ORDERING RULE, and refused to ratchet. Three
// places, stated because pillar 4 asks for the refusals as loudly as the ratchets:
//
//   1. **Acquire and release are a symmetric pair within a mode.** There is no
//      `read.acquire` -> `read.used` -> `read.release` chain. Taking a read lock
//      and releasing it without reading anything is a legitimate idiom — it is
//      how you wait for a writer to finish — and `rwlock.c` asserts nothing about
//      it. The gate that exists is on the LOCK OBJECT (`hold.skipped`), never on
//      an individual critical section. Ratcheting the pair would be ceremony.
//   2. **Nothing counts.** `uk_rwlock_rlock` admits N concurrent readers and
//      `nactive` counts them. A phantom state cannot count, and pretending
//      otherwise by minting `reading1`/`reading2` would be a lie about what is
//      proven. One handle here means one holder; see README.md.
//   3. **No failure arms on the four hot tors.** `rlock`, `wlock`, `runlock` and
//      `wunlock` all return `void`. They cannot fail — they block. So the hot
//      path is four bare-return tors with no branch, no payload and no state
//      beyond the one that carries the mode. The brief's rule that obligation-on-
//      failure scales inversely with resource frequency lands here as: the rare
//      thing (`create`, once per lock) has the failure arm, the frequent thing
//      (every critical section) has none, and that is not a choice this lift
//      made — it is what the C signatures already say.
//
// WHAT `create` DOES NOT OFFER. `uk_rwlock_init_config`'s `config_flags` has
// exactly one defined bit, `UK_RWLOCK_CONFIG_WRITE_RECURSE` (`rwlock.h:24`), and
// `rwlock.c:23` asserts it is NOT set — "the implementation does not support
// recursive write locking", in the C's own TODO. With asserts off that assertion
// is `do {} while(0)` and the flag is accepted, at which point the second `wlock`
// waits for `nactive == 0` while holding `nactive == -1`: a self-deadlock. This
// module has no parameter for it. `create` always passes 0. The flag is not
// refused at run time; it is unspellable.

// ============================================================================
// UNIKRAFT C ABI
// ============================================================================
//
// Every symbol below is on `lib/uklock/exportsyms.uk`. Nothing in this module
// reaches a `static inline`, so the archive links against a stock unikernel with
// no shim, no wrapper and no extra call frame.

extern fn uk_rwlock_init_config(rwl: *Lock, config_flags: c_uint) void;
extern fn uk_rwlock_rlock(rwl: *Lock) void;
extern fn uk_rwlock_wlock(rwl: *Lock) void;
extern fn uk_rwlock_runlock(rwl: *Lock) void;
extern fn uk_rwlock_wunlock(rwl: *Lock) void;

// nolibc, backed by ukalloc's default allocator. `struct __align(8) uk_rwlock`
// (rwlock.h:27) is 8-aligned, so the storage comes from `memalign`.
extern fn memalign(alignment: usize, size: usize) ?*anyopaque;
extern fn free(ptr: ?*anyopaque) void;

// ============================================================================
// THE MIRROR, AND THE PROOF THAT REPLACES THE GUESS
// ============================================================================
//
// `uklock` exports no allocator and no sizing symbol: `struct uk_rwlock` is a
// caller-provided object, so a lift MUST know how big it is. That is an ABI
// claim, and the shelf's own history says an ABI claim that is wrong corrupts
// memory silently. `unikraft/blk` guards its mirrors with `@offsetOf` — which
// proves the mirror is self-consistent and says nothing about the C.
// `unikraft/pages` interrogates the C at run time instead, and that is the
// pattern this module follows.
//
// THE SIZE IS NOT MIRRORED. IT IS MEASURED. That is not stylistic caution — a
// mirrored `sizeof` was written first, booted, and was WRONG by 24 bytes:
//
//     struct uk_waitq { struct uk_list_head waiters; __spinlock lock; };
//
// and `__spinlock` is `struct __spinlock { volatile int lock; }` under
// `CONFIG_HAVE_SMP` and `typedef struct __spinlock { /* empty */ }` — SIZE ZERO
// — without it (`include/uk/arch/spinlock.h:35` vs `:97`). A single-core image,
// which is the default and is what boots here, therefore has a 48-byte
// `struct uk_rwlock` where an SMP image has 72. `uklock`'s own Kconfig does not
// mention SMP, `rwlock.h` does not mention it, and no symbol reports it. A lift
// that transcribed the header and asserted `@sizeOf == 72` would have been
// self-consistently, confidently wrong in every image anyone actually builds.
//
// So this module mirrors ONLY THE FIELDS IT READS, and those are the four
// leading `int`-sized counters whose offsets the C ABI fixes on every target
// Unikraft supports. Everything from offset 16 down — the spinlock and the two
// wait queues — is never named here and never assumed; its extent is measured by
// `probe` on the running image.
const Lock = extern struct {
    nactive: c_int, // 0   -1 == a writer holds it; n > 0 == n readers
    npending_reads: c_uint, // 4
    npending_writes: c_uint, // 8
    config_flags: c_uint, // 12
    // Below here: `struct uk_spinlock sl`, `struct uk_waitq shared`,
    // `struct uk_waitq exclusive` (rwlock.h:33-37). Not mirrored. Not read.
};

comptime {
    if (@offsetOf(Lock, "nactive") != 0) @compileError("uk_rwlock.nactive moved");
    if (@offsetOf(Lock, "npending_reads") != 4) @compileError("uk_rwlock.npending_reads moved");
    if (@offsetOf(Lock, "npending_writes") != 8) @compileError("uk_rwlock.npending_writes moved");
    if (@offsetOf(Lock, "config_flags") != 12) @compileError("uk_rwlock.config_flags moved");
}

// `struct __align(8) uk_rwlock` — rwlock.h:23. The alignment IS in the header,
// spelled out, and unlike the size it does not move with a Kconfig option. It is
// the one layout number this module takes from the source rather than from the
// running image, and the partial mirror above is only 4-aligned, so it cannot be
// derived from `@alignOf`.
const ALIGN: usize = 8;

// The byte the probe paints over the whole scratch allocation before handing it
// to the C. Any value works; 0xA5 is chosen because it is neither 0 nor a
// plausible pointer byte, so a stale read of it is obvious in a memory dump.
const CANARY: u8 = 0xA5;

// The probe's scratch block, and the untouched tail it insists on finding.
//
// The point of the guard is CONTAINMENT: a `struct uk_rwlock` too big for this
// lift's expectations writes INSIDE the scratch block rather than past it, so
// the mismatch is detected where it would otherwise have been the silent
// corruption of whatever the allocator handed out next. Neither number is a
// claim about any layout. SCRATCH is how much room the measurement gets; GUARD
// is how much of it must come back untouched before the measurement is believed.
const SCRATCH: usize = 512;
const GUARD: usize = 64;

// An undefined config bit, used once as an offset witness. `rwlock.h:24` defines
// exactly one flag, `UK_RWLOCK_CONFIG_WRITE_RECURSE = 0x01`, and
// `uk_rwlock_is_write_recursive` masks bit 0 only. Bit 1 therefore travels from
// `init`'s argument into `config_flags` and is read by nothing — which makes it
// the one field below the first word whose offset the C can be made to confirm.
const WITNESS_FLAG: c_uint = 0x02;

// ============================================================================
// THE CAPABILITY
// ============================================================================

// The measured `struct uk_rwlock` of THIS image, and the proof that it was
// measured rather than assumed. `create` is the only source of a `*Lock` and it
// needs one of these, so nothing in this module can run before the measurement
// has.
const Locks = extern struct {
    bytes: usize, // what `create` allocates — derived, see `probe`
    wrote: usize, // the highest byte `uk_rwlock_init_config` touched, + 1
};

var locks: Locks = undefined;

// ============================================================================
// MEASURE THE LAYOUT
// ============================================================================

// Hand the C a canary-painted block, watch what it writes, and refuse unless
// what comes back is `rwlock.c`'s behaviour. Five tests, each with its own
// refusal:
//
//   1. SCRATCH. `memalign(8, 512)` succeeds and all 512 bytes are painted 0xA5.
//   2. THE EXTENT IS MEASURED. After `uk_rwlock_init_config`, scan down from the
//      top for the highest byte that is no longer the canary. That index plus
//      one is how far the C's own initializer reached, and it is where the size
//      of `struct uk_rwlock` comes from — not from a header, not from a
//      `@sizeOf`. `uk_rwlock_init_config` writes every field the struct has
//      (rwlock.c:15-27: four stores, `uk_spin_init`, two `uk_waitq_init`), so
//      the last byte it touches is the last byte of the last field.
//   3. THE MEASUREMENT IS CONTAINED. At least GUARD bytes at the top of the
//      scratch block are still canary, and the measurement is non-zero. A zero
//      says the linked symbol wrote nothing; a measurement running into the
//      guard says the struct is bigger than this lift budgeted room to measure,
//      and both are refused rather than rounded off. Because the guard is INSIDE
//      the allocation, an oversized struct is detected here instead of silently
//      corrupting whatever the allocator handed out next.
//   4. THE FLAG LANDS AT 12. Init with `0x02`, an undefined bit that
//      `uk_rwlock_is_write_recursive` does not mask and nothing else reads, then
//      read `config_flags` back through the partial mirror. This is the only
//      offset below the first word that the C can be made to confirm, and with
//      the three counters reading zero it pins all four mirrored fields.
//   5. `nactive` MOVES THE WAY rwlock.c SAYS. Re-init with 0, then walk
//      rlock -> runlock -> wlock -> wunlock and read offset 0 after each:
//      0, 1, 0, -1, 0. `wlock`'s -1 is the strongest single witness in the set —
//      it is a value no other lock primitive in Unikraft produces, so seeing it
//      proves the symbols this archive linked are `rwlock.c`'s and not something
//      else that happened to satisfy the name.
//
// Test 5 costs four real lock operations at start-up, once per image. They
// cannot block: `_uk_waitq_wait_until` (wait.h:106) is `while (!(condition))`,
// and on a lock nobody else holds every condition is already true, so the
// scheduler is never entered. A program calls `probe` once and threads the
// `*Locks`.
//
// There is no degraded path. If any test fails, `probe` takes `| unsupported`
// naming which one, and because `create` is the only source of a `*Lock` and it
// needs the `*Locks`, nothing in this module can be used at all. There is no
// "assume 72", and the first draft of this file proves why there must not be:
// it assumed 72, and 48 is the answer on a single-core image.
~pub tor probe { }
| ready { locks: *Locks, bytes: u64, wrote: u64, witness: string }
| unsupported string

~proc probe|zig {
    const raw = memalign(ALIGN, SCRATCH) orelse {
        return .{ .unsupported = "out of memory allocating the layout-probe scratch block" };
    };
    const bytes: [*]u8 = @ptrCast(raw);
    for (0..SCRATCH) |i| bytes[i] = CANARY;

    const m: *Lock = @ptrCast(@alignCast(raw));

    uk_rwlock_init_config(m, WITNESS_FLAG);

    // Test 2 — how far the initializer reached.
    var wrote: usize = SCRATCH;
    while (wrote > 0 and bytes[wrote - 1] == CANARY) : (wrote -= 1) {}

    // Test 3 — the measurement is contained, at both ends.
    if (wrote == 0) {
        free(raw);
        return .{ .unsupported = "uk_rwlock_init_config wrote nothing: the linked symbol is not lib/uklock's initializer" };
    }
    if (wrote + GUARD > SCRATCH) {
        free(raw);
        return .{ .unsupported = "struct uk_rwlock is larger than this lift budgeted room to measure: uk_rwlock_init_config reached into the probe's guard region, so its extent is not established and no lock is handed out" };
    }

    // Test 4 — config_flags is at offset 12 and the counters at 0, 4 and 8.
    if (m.config_flags != WITNESS_FLAG) {
        free(raw);
        return .{ .unsupported = "struct uk_rwlock.config_flags is not at offset 12: the value handed to uk_rwlock_init_config did not come back out of the mirrored field" };
    }
    if (m.nactive != 0 or m.npending_reads != 0 or m.npending_writes != 0) {
        free(raw);
        return .{ .unsupported = "uk_rwlock_init_config did not zero the three counters at offsets 0, 4 and 8" };
    }

    // Test 5 — the identity witness. Re-initialize with the flag this module
    // actually uses, then watch nactive.
    uk_rwlock_init_config(m, 0);
    const at_init = m.nactive;
    uk_rwlock_rlock(m);
    const at_rlock = m.nactive;
    uk_rwlock_runlock(m);
    const at_runlock = m.nactive;
    uk_rwlock_wlock(m);
    const at_wlock = m.nactive;
    uk_rwlock_wunlock(m);
    const at_wunlock = m.nactive;
    free(raw);

    if (at_init != 0 or at_rlock != 1 or at_runlock != 0 or at_wlock != -1 or at_wunlock != 0) {
        return .{ .unsupported = "the linked uk_rwlock_* symbols do not drive nactive the way rwlock.c does: expected 0, 1, 0, -1, 0 across init/rlock/runlock/wlock/wunlock" };
    }

    // Rounded up to the alignment `rwlock.h:23` declares. Trailing padding is by
    // definition never written, so the scan cannot see it — and rounding up is
    // what covers it. At most ALIGN-1 bytes wider than the struct, never
    // narrower than anything the C touches.
    const size = (wrote + ALIGN - 1) & ~(ALIGN - 1);
    locks = Locks{ .bytes = size, .wrote = wrote };
    return .{ .ready = .{
        .locks = &locks,
        .bytes = @intCast(size),
        .wrote = @intCast(wrote),
        .witness = "nactive: init 0, rlock 1, runlock 0, wlock -1, wunlock 0",
    } };
}

// ============================================================================
// THE LOCK
// ============================================================================

// `uk_rwlock_init_config(l, 0)` over storage this module owns. Mints <fresh!>.
//
// A caller never sees a `struct uk_rwlock *` and never supplies its storage,
// which is the whole reason the C's five `UK_ASSERT(rwl)` sites have nothing
// left to catch: the pointer is minted here, from a checked allocation, and the
// only way to name one is to hold the handle this branch produces.
//
// The flags argument is not exposed. See the header note on
// `UK_RWLOCK_CONFIG_WRITE_RECURSE`.
//
// One failure arm with a reason rather than a state: an allocation that did not
// happen leaves nothing to hand back, so there is no obligation to carry.
~pub tor create { locks: *Locks }
| lock *Lock<fresh!>
| unavailable string

~proc create|zig {
    const raw = memalign(ALIGN, locks.bytes) orelse {
        return .{ .unavailable = "out of memory allocating a uk_rwlock" };
    };
    const l: *Lock = @ptrCast(@alignCast(raw));
    uk_rwlock_init_config(l, 0);
    return .{ .lock = l };
}

// `uk_rwlock_rlock`. Mints <reading!>.
//
// Accepts <!fresh|!idle> — a lock that is initialized and not currently held by
// this program. It does NOT accept <!reading>, so recursive read locking cannot
// be spelled. That refusal is not transcribed from an assertion, because there
// is none: `rlock` twice on one thread is silently legal in the C, sets
// `nactive` to 2, and deadlocks the moment a writer arrives between the two
// calls — the second `rlock` waits on `npending_writes > 0` (rwlock.c:53) while
// the writer waits on `nactive == 0` (rwlock.c:76), and neither can move. The C
// has a comment about reader/writer starvation at rwlock.c:41-50 and no check.
//
// No failure arm: `uk_rwlock_rlock` returns void. It blocks or it succeeds.
~pub tor read.acquire { lock: *Lock<!fresh|!idle> } -> *Lock<reading!>

~proc read.acquire|zig {
    uk_rwlock_rlock(lock);
    return lock;
}

// `uk_rwlock_runlock`. Accepts ONLY <!reading>, which is the lift of
// rwlock.c:98 (`UK_ASSERT(rwl->nactive > 0)`).
//
// A <writing!> handle does not type-check here, so the -1 -> -2 corruption
// described in the header is unspellable rather than asserted. A second
// `read.release` does not type-check either: this tor consumes the <!reading>
// and mints <idle!>.
~pub tor read.release { lock: *Lock<!reading> } -> *Lock<idle!>

~proc read.release|zig {
    uk_rwlock_runlock(lock);
    return lock;
}

// `uk_rwlock_wlock`. Mints <writing!>.
//
// Accepts <!fresh|!idle> and NOT <!writing>, so recursive write locking cannot
// be spelled. The C's position on that is contradictory and worth stating:
// `rwlock.h:24` defines a flag to enable it, `rwlock.c:22` calls it a TODO, and
// `rwlock.c:23` asserts the flag is off — which in a shipped image is
// `do {} while(0)`. So the C offers the feature, does not implement it, and
// stops warning about it in exactly the builds people run. Here the flag has no
// spelling and the second `wlock` has no type.
//
// No failure arm: `uk_rwlock_wlock` returns void.
~pub tor write.acquire { lock: *Lock<!fresh|!idle> } -> *Lock<writing!>

~proc write.acquire|zig {
    uk_rwlock_wlock(lock);
    return lock;
}

// `uk_rwlock_wunlock`. Accepts ONLY <!writing>, which is the lift of
// rwlock.c:120 (`UK_ASSERT(rwl->nactive == -1)`).
//
// This is the arm of the pair that fails SILENTLY in C rather than loudly: a
// `runlock`-shaped mistake hangs the next acquirer, but calling `wunlock` while
// holding a read lock takes `nactive` from 1 to 0 and admits a writer alongside
// the reader still inside. No hang, no assert in a shipped image, a data race
// that reproduces on someone else's machine.
~pub tor write.release { lock: *Lock<!writing> } -> *Lock<idle!>

~proc write.release|zig {
    uk_rwlock_wunlock(lock);
    return lock;
}

// THE NAMED ESCAPE from the asymmetry gate. `destroy` accepts only <!idle>, and
// only a release mints it, so a lock that was created and never taken cannot be
// destroyed. That is the point — but a lock created on a path that turns out not
// to need it is legitimate, and this is how a program says so out loud.
//
// Deliberately greppable: `grep -r "lock:hold.skipped"` answers "which programs
// build a lock and never enter it", which in a unikernel is a question about a
// live allocation AND about whether the critical section it was written for
// still exists.
// It runs no C call, which is the honest emission — nothing happened.
~pub tor hold.skipped { lock: *Lock<!fresh> } -> *Lock<idle!>

~proc hold.skipped|zig {
    return lock;
}

// The storage goes back. Accepts ONLY <!idle> and consumes it.
//
// There is NO C call here, and that is not an omission: `uklock` has no
// destructor for a `struct uk_rwlock`. It is a plain object with two intrusive
// wait queues, and Unikraft's own answer to "how do I dispose of one" is that
// you do not — every rwlock in the tree is a static or lives inside a longer
// lived struct. The only thing owed is the allocation `create` made, so that is
// the only thing this tor does.
//
// Destroying a lock a thread is still waiting on would be a real bug and this
// module cannot catch it: the waiters are other threads, and no phantom state
// on this handle knows about them. Stated as a claim not made, in README.md.
//
// No failure arm: `free` returns void and cannot fail.
~pub tor destroy { lock: *Lock<!idle> }

~proc destroy|zig {
    free(@ptrCast(lock));
}
